In article <3802@uwm.edu> jong@csd4.csd.uwm.edu (Jon C Glueckstein) writes:
>A related method for obtaining help when wishing would be to
>introduce some new items to the game.  How about a 'Book of
>Sword Lore' which could be obtained for the price of one
>wish?  Of course, there would be different books for different
>classes of weapons.
>
>By reading this book (possibly even scroll), a list of
>(possibly all) weapons of a certain class is displayed.

No, make it intrinsic to the game.

In other words, reading a scroll of sword lore will enable the
menu method for swords only:

"What do you want to wish for? [ab *]"
*
a - sword
b - other
--more--
"What do you want to wish for? [ab *]"
a

(BTW - SPOILER list of swords follows)

"What do you want to wish for? [abcdefghijklmno *]"
*
a - butcher knife
b - short sword
c - elven short sword
d - orcish short sword
e - dwarvish short sword
f - broadsword
g - elven broadsword
h - double-edged blade
i - dwarvish mattock
j - fencing foil
k - katana
l - long sword
m - scimitar
n - two-handed sword
o - other
--more--
"What do you want to wish for? [abcdefghijklmno *]"

etc. (menus for blessings, +s, name)

Reading the scroll of sword lore doesn't reveal all the names.  You
don't get the names till you actually use the wish.  Even then, you
will only see names that apply to the final weapon you choose.  If you
choose a type of weapon that has no named versions of it then you will
just get the weapon without seeing any names.

This has the added benefit of making wishes more complicated, and giving
adventurers more attributes to acquire ("learn the lore before you wish",
says the oracle).

